```
Wait, the LocalHostServer constructor creates the HttpServer which binds → BindException thrown inside the constructor. Hmm, but `relayStore` was created *before* the server. So when the constructor throws, we jump to the outer `catch (IOException | RuntimeException failure)` which closes `composition` — but **not relayStore**! That's the leak: the relay store connection stays open, holding host.db-wal, so `TemporaryWorkspace.close()` can't delete it.

Wait, but that's the third open. Actually the failing open throws BindException from the constructor → outer catch closes composition only. My relayStore leaks. Yes! That's the bug — my resource leak in an error path.

Fix: wrap the relay store creation and the server construction in an inner try/catch that closes the relay store. Restructure:

```java
var relayStore = new SqliteRelaySettingsStore(dataDirectory.resolve("host.db"));
try {
    var server = new LocalHostServer(...);
    try {
        ...
        return new LocalHostApplication(composition, server, ipc, relayStore);
    } catch (RuntimeException failure) { server.close(); throw failure; }
} catch (RuntimeException failure) {
    relayStore.close();
    throw failure;
}
```
But the outer catch already handles composition. Let me restructure carefully: put relayStore creation inside the outer try, and add relayStore.close() to the outer catch too. But relayStore must be effectively final & in scope in the catch — it's declared inside the try block, so the catch can't see it. Use a local variable declared before the try:

```java
try {
    var tokens = ...;
    ...
    var relayStore = new SqliteRelaySettingsStore(...);
    try {
        var server = new LocalHostServer(...);
        try { ... } catch (RuntimeException f) { server.close(); throw f; }
    } catch (RuntimeException failure) {
        relayStore.close();
        throw failure;
    }
} catch (IOException | RuntimeException failure) { composition.close(); throw failure; }
```

Hmm, but `relayStore.open` itself can throw (migration failure) → then relayStore isn't assigned; the inner try wouldn't catch it. Put the store creation as the first statement of the inner try and use a holder... Simpler:

```java
var relayStore = new SqliteRelaySettingsStore(dataDirectory.resolve("host.db"));
try {
    ... server + everything ...
} catch (RuntimeException failure) {
    relayStore.close();
    throw failure;
}
```
and keep the inner `server` close. So:

```java
var server = new LocalHostServer(...);
try {
    relayStore.current().ifPresent(server::applyRelay);
    ...
    return new LocalHostApplication(...);
} catch (RuntimeException failure) {
    server.close();
    relayStore.close();
    throw failure;
}
```
But the LocalHostServer constructor throwing (BindException) happens outside that try. So wrap BOTH:

```java
var relayStore = new SqliteRelaySettingsStore(dataDirectory.resolve("host.db"));
try {
    var server = new LocalHostServer(...);
    try {
        ...
        return new LocalHostApplication(composition, server, ipc, relayStore);
    } catch (RuntimeException failure) {
        server.close();
        throw failure;
    }
} catch (RuntimeException failure) {
    relayStore.close();
    throw failure;
}
```
That covers everything. Note: if the LocalHostApplication is returned successfully, neither catch runs. Good.

